line miles, including overhead pole line and underground conduit carrying primary, secondary, and street lighting circuits. The company serves customers in Ohio, Pennsylvania, New Jersey, West Virginia, Maryland, and New York. FirstEnergy Corp. was incorporated in 1996 and is headquartered in Akron, Ohio.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Brian X. Tierney | CEO, President & Chairman | Age 57 | Compensation: $3.9M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 11K people. Revenue per employee of $1.3M ranks in the above the sector median among Utilities peers. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["FirstEnergy Corp. is a large-cap utilities business with a $27.2B market cap on $15.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +7% over the past three years and +0% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 5% against a 5% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The company is burning through $1.0B of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 6.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 3.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Price sits 27% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.26709475275034056},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 7.1% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Payout ratio of 88% leaves thin coverage if earnings wobble","sev":"amber","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Utilities peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the inevitable bad year","sev":"amber","cat":"balance_sheet"},{"text":"Regulatory rate-case risk and rising cost of capital \u2014 the allowed return is set by commissions, and the business lives or dies by that negotiation.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Grid modernisation capex burden \u2014 aging infrastructure requires massive reinvestment that regulators may not fully allow into rate base, compressing earned returns.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Wildfire and climate liability \u2014 extreme weather events create catastrophic, uninsurable losses that can push even investment-grade utilities toward restructuring.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["28 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"FirstEnergy Corporation $FE Stock: Illinois Municipal Retirement Fund Grows Position","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/12/firstenergy-corporation-fe-stock-illinois-municipal-retirement-fund-grows-position.html","date":"2026-11-12","timestamp":1794481780.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.836,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Can FirstEnergy (NYSE:FE) Build On Its Stronger Quarter?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/utility/can-firstenergy-nysefe-build-on-its-stronger-quarter","date":"2026-11-08","timestamp":17941614